Take the relation length off the extension lock.

Extending a relation under zou wrote the fork's new length to the
store from the backend, four bytes, synchronously, inside the relation
extension lock every other backend wanting to extend the same relation
was queued on. On server3 with a MinIO store that is 1696 puts of four
bytes in a 45 second insert run, against 3187 puts for the WAL itself,
each a network round trip at a 16 ms p50, and Lock/extend is 45 per
cent of the writing backends' time, the largest single wait.

The length a running cluster reads already comes from the shared table
0004 added, so the store write is only there for what a restart reads.
Replay covers most of that by itself: a record touching a block past
the end extends the relation to cover it. What replay cannot rebuild
is a length whose records are below the redo floor, so an extension
now marks its entry dirty and CheckPointGuts writes every dirty length
to the store, inside the checkpoint that is about to move that floor.

Truncate and unlink still write and delete the SIZE object eagerly and
now take a flush lock while they do, so a checkpoint's put cannot land
on top of a length that was just lowered or a fork that was just
dropped. A backend outside a postmaster has no shared table to leave a
length in and keeps writing it on the spot, as does one that finds the
table full.

